> > It follows unix philosophy, as defined by ESR: a large set of
> > well-defined, simple tools that, when interacting, create complex results.
>
> *HOW* does Nethack follow the Unix philosophy?

Nethack doesn't have one big EMACS wand that does everything if you
hold it with your fingers in the right places.  It has a long list
of wands that each do something different.  Same with amulets,
weapons, armor, food, potions, scrolls, tools and to a lesser
extent gems.

In Unix when you chain commands together you get results whose
value depend on your skills.  In Nethack when you chain potions
together you get alchemy, when you chain certain potions with
certain scrolls you get certain results, etc, usw.

